{
 "title": "The GT-Score: A Robust Objective Function for Reducing Overfitting in Data-Driven Trading Strategies",
 "hypothesis": "GT-Score composite objective (mu * ln(z) * r2 / sigma_d with z=(mu-mu_m)/(sigma/sqrt(N)), piecewise penalty, r2 consistency, sigma_d downside) improves generalization ratio (OOS return / train return) vs Simple, Sharpe, Sortino under identical search budget on daily equity backtests.",
 "data_sources": [
  "OHLCV via yFinance API https://pypi.org/project/yfinance/ accessed on 8 January 2026"
 ],
 "sample": "Historical daily OHLCV for top 50 S&P 500 by market cap via yFinance. Span January 2010 through December 2024, approximately 3770 trading days per asset, covering post-2008 recovery, 2020 COVID-19 crash, 2022 rate environment. 3 strategies: RSI (wilder:1978b), MACD (appel:1979), Bollinger (bollinger:2001). Monte Carlo study: 50 assets * 4 loss functions * 3 strategies * 15 seeds = 9000 trials. Walk-forward validation: 50 assets * 4 losses * 3 strategies * 9 splits with embargo, paper prints 5340 sequential trials, 1335 per loss, validation periods 2014--2024. Supplementary code and processed result files provided.",
 "period_start": "January 2010",
 "period_end": "December 2024",
 "method": [
  "1. Load daily OHLCV for 50 largest market-cap S&P500 via yFinance for Jan 2010-Dec 2024 (~3770 bars/asset).",
  "2. Define parameter spaces for RSI (overbought/oversold thresholds), MACD (fast, slow, signal periods), Bollinger (lookback window, band width).",
  "3. Walk-forward splits: Training window = 4 years, Validation window = 2 years, Step size = 1 year, Embargo = 30 days between train and validation. Generate 9 sequential train/validation splits per asset covering validation 2014--2024.",
  "4. Monte Carlo: For each of 50*4*3 configurations run 15 random seeds 42--56 inclusive.",
  "5. Optimization: Random search with 25 parameter evaluations per trial, same budget for all 4 objectives. Author dissertation also tested TPE and genetic algorithms.",
  "6. Objectives compared: Simple L_simple=-Profit; Sharpe L_sharpe=-Sharpe; Sortino L_sortino=-Sortino; GT-Score (see 7).",
  "7. Compute GT-Score per backtest window: GT_Score = mu*ln(z)*r2 / sigma_d. Where mu=mean strategy return per observation, mu_m=mean buy-and-hold benchmark per observation, sigma=std of strategy returns, N=number of return observations (trade-level returns, N = number of executed trades), z=(mu-mu_m)/(sigma/sqrt(N)), ln(z)=natural log, r2=R-squared consistency of returns, sigma_d=downside deviation.",
  "8. Edge handling piecewise with epsilon=10^{-6} to prevent /0 when sigma_d=0: if z<=0 then score=100+100*(1-exp(-|z-1|)) large penalty; else if z<=1 then score=100*(1-exp(-|z-1|)) smooth transition; else score= - mu*ln(z)*r2/(sigma_d+epsilon) standard GT-Score (negated for minimization).",
  "9. Minimum sample n_min=50 trades: parameterizations with <50 trades assign large penalty. Report mode uses fixed-threshold n_min=50. Stabilized variance option (dissertation, code supported but NOT used for reported results): partition equity curve into n equal periods, choose n* where variance of period returns changes <1% across recent candidates, fallback to n=50 if no plateau.",
  "10. Backtest engine Python 3.10 custom daily-bar backtest, no instrument-specific execution modeling in main tables. Compute Train Mean return, Test/Validation Mean return, Test Std, Train/Val Std, Gen Ratio = Test Return / Train Return (Walk-forward: Val Return / Train Return).",
  "11. Summarize Monte Carlo OOS performance by loss (N=2250 per loss) and Walk-forward performance by loss (N=1335 per loss). Compute improvement % vs baseline avg.",
  "12. Statistical comparisons on Monte Carlo test returns paired across same asset/strategy/seed: paired t-test GT-Score vs each baseline reporting Mean Diff (GT minus baseline), t-Stat, p-Value, Cohen d, Sig stars (* p<0.05, *** p<0.001). Also Wilcoxon signed-rank paired non-parametric p. Note author treats p-values as descriptive not selection-corrected (no White Reality Check, Hansen SPA, DSR); emphasize effect sizes d<0.2 and walk-forward Gen Ratio as primary evidence.",
  "13. Sensitivity: transaction-cost check subtract additional per-side cost proportional to test-window trades, range 0--10 bps per side (entry and exit). Record mean test-window trades 32.4 GT vs ~21.0 baselines. Plot monotone decline, check ranking stability."
 ],
